WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:16.500
An RTOS application is a collection of cooperating parts: tasks, scheduler behavior, kernel services, timers, interrupt handlers, queues, semaphores, mutexes, drivers, peripheral interfaces, and shared resources.

2
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:22.440
Security review begins by understanding how those parts interact and where assumptions change.

3
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.520
Tasks provide structure for concurrent behavior.

4
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:34.180
One task might read a sensor, another might update control state, another might manage communication, and another might handle diagnostics.

5
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:40.440
Each task has a priority, stack, lifecycle, resource access pattern, and communication model.

6
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.140
Those details define how much authority the task has.

7
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.700
Kernel services mediate scheduling and coordination.

8
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.400
Queues carry messages.

9
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.660
Semaphores and mutexes coordinate access.

10
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.560
Timers shape behavior over time.

11
00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:03.380
These services are not security controls by themselves, but they influence how data and authority move through the system.

12
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.280
A queue from an external-facing task to a control task is a boundary worth reviewing.

13
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.880
Interrupts and drivers have different constraints from ordinary task code.

14
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:17.540
Interrupt context may run at sensitive times and should usually do minimal work.

15
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.240
Drivers may bridge hardware state into application state.

16
00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:27.740
Peripheral input can be stale, malformed, out of range, or inconsistent with the current mode.

17
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.160
Driver boundaries should be explicit.

18
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.160
Privileged code should be easy to identify.

19
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:43.500
Where the RTOS or processor supports privilege levels, memory protection, or restricted peripheral access, the design should show which code uses that authority and why.

20
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:50.880
Even when hardware isolation is limited, teams can still document logical boundaries and review high-authority code more carefully.

21
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.120
A boundary map improves review quality.

22
00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:05.660
It helps reviewers see where data crosses from hardware to driver, from interrupt to task, from communication stack to control logic, and from diagnostic mode to production behavior.

23
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.840
Once boundaries are visible, validation, logging, and failure behavior can be placed intentionally.
